course: dictionary
lesson: 15579
title: Swept: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Swept is the past form of sweep. Sweep, sweeps, swept and sweeping are all the same word, used at different times. «I swept the kitchen floor yesterday.» Because it happened yesterday, the word is swept.
explain: Sweep can mean clean with a brush or broom. It can also mean move quickly and smoothly, or push something along with force. «The flood swept the cars down the hill.» The water was so strong that it carried the cars away.
explain: Context tells you which meaning to choose. Ask yourself: is this a chore, a flood, or a news story? «A storm swept across the country» means it spread fast over a big area. Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
fact: Swept is the past form of sweep.
fact: «Swept» can mean cleaned with a brush or broom, as in «She swept the path».
fact: «Swept» can mean carried along with force, as in «The wave swept the sandcastle away».
fact: «Swept» can mean spread fast over an area, as in «The news swept through the school».
fact: Safety first: a strong current can sweep a swimmer out, so swim only between the flags.
exercise: «I swept the kitchen floor after tea.» What does swept mean here? | Went to bed early | Painted the walls | Ate all the crumbs | *Cleaned with a brush
exercise: «The flood swept the cars down the hill.» What does swept mean here? | Stopped suddenly | Dried out in the sun | Parked neatly in a row | *Carried along with great force
exercise: «A storm swept across the country.» What does swept suggest? | Sat quietly in one place | *Spread fast over a big area | Turned into snow | Grew smaller and softer
test: «The team swept every game and won the cup.» What does swept mean? | Cleaned the pitch | Played in the rain | *Won every game easily | Lost every game
test: Which word is the past form of sweep? | *Swept | Sweeping | Sweeps
exam: «The current swept her out to sea.» What should swimmers do to stay safe? | Wait for the current to sweep them back | *Swim only between the flags | Swim as far out as they like | Sweep the sand before they swim
